Mind, experience, and subjectivity (@mind-experience-and-subjectivity: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_47VCQR824JH7PZ2VNNSKCH6B7K
Philosophy of mind examines consciousness, perception, emotion, thought, embodiment, personal identity, and the relation between mind and world. This material compares first-person description, conceptual argument, and scientific explanation, including phenomenological approaches and debates about physicalism, representation, and situated experience. Reuse relevant neuroscience and cognitive science from [ERG-104](../../../ergon/gaia/life/neuroscience-and-cognitive-science/Neuroscience-and-cognitive-science.lingua#r_13CGJGRFBS2ACJ69T0KCP7GZAQ) as evidence and context, with the philosophical assumptions visible. Literary accounts of experience can support comparison without being treated as experimental observations.

**Related plans:** [Neuroscience and cognitive science](../../../ergon/gaia/life/neuroscience-and-cognitive-science/Neuroscience-and-cognitive-science.lingua#r_13CGJGRFBS2ACJ69T0KCP7GZAQ).

